er tiles.\n\nConsidering the seat wind is South (F2), and I have F3 and F4. Prevailing wind is West (F4). So keeping F4 is beneficial. Discarding F4 would lose the chance for a Prevalent Wind pung.\n\nTherefore, the best move might be to discard F3 or J2. Between them, F3 is not the seat or prevailing wind, so discarding F3 is better. Similarly, J2 is a single tile. Let's see the valid actions: Play F3 is an option. So playing F3 would be a good choice, as it's a single honor tile with no immediate use and not part of any pung.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The current hand has a concealed kong of W8, contributing 4 points. The remaining tiles include a mix of suits and honors. Discarding F3, a single honor tile not part of any pung, minimizes the loss of potential scoring opportunities while preserving the chance to form a Prevalent Wind pung with F4.  \nReason: F3 is a single honor tile with no immediate use for pungs or chows. Discarding it avoids blocking potential patterns like Prevalent Wind (F4) and maintains flexibility for future melds.  \nAnswer: Play F3"}
